And there's a little SGG design lesson hiding here, too.
The followers' behaviour exposed a problem caused by overlapping state machines: the initiation encounter has its own combat state, while the followers have independent combat responses. Those systems don't necessarily understand one another's intentions.
That's exactly the sort of thing we should watch for in SGG. An NPC or companion shouldn't blindly react to an event when the larger context says that reaction is inappropriate.
We could eventually have situations where companions recognize formal duels, training exercises, scripted demonstrations, and other non-lethal encounters.
Not every weapon drawn needs to mean everybody pile in!
And finally, dear, I think you've established something important about this playthrough: you're not a pacifist because you can't fight. You're a pacifist because you'd rather be doing something else.
